` — Set of item types that have block pricing\n- `BLOCK_PRICE` — Price for a block (exactly 3)\n- `EXACT_BLOCK_COUNT` — The count needed for block pricing\n- `PROCESSING_FEE` — Flat processing fee added to every quote\n\n**Assessment**:\n- `processScenario` — Clear. Processes a scenario with multiple steps.\n- `calculateBasePremium` — Clear but could be more precise. It calculates the \"base\" (before processing fee) but does include surcharges (cursed, enchantment). The word \"base\" is slightly misleading since it includes surcharges. However, given that it excludes the processing fee and loyalty/first-insurance modifiers (not yet implemented), \"base\" is a reasonable term for \"before fee and policy-level modifiers.\"\n- `ITEM_PRICES` — Clear.\n- All constants are well-named.\n- The function structure reveals intent: `calculateBasePremium` computes base, `processScenario` wraps with processing fee.\n\n**Decision**: Names are good. `calculateBasePremium` is fine — it represents the pre-fee, pre-policy-modifier price. No renaming needed.\n\n## Initial APP Mass Calculation\n\nLet me calculate the mass of `calculateBasePremium` (the core function):\n\n```\nconst calculateBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number => {\n  const counts: Record<string, number> = {};             // binding: counts (1)\n  let total = 0;                                          // binding: total (1), constant: 0 (1), assignment: total=0 (6)\n  for (const item of items) {                             // loop: for...of (5), binding: item (1)\n    counts[item.type] = (counts[item.type] || 0) + 1;    // assignment: counts[...]= (6), invocation: || (2), constant: 0 (1), constant: 1 (1)\n    if (BLOCK_PRICE_COMPONENTS.has(item.type)) continue;  // conditional: if (4), invocation: .has() (2)\n    let itemBase = ITEM_PRICES[item.type] || 0;           // binding: itemBase (1), invocation: || (2), constant: 0 (1), assignment: itemBase= (6)\n    if (item.cursed) {                                    // con