m\" is still accurate — it computes the base before policy-wide modifiers. **Keep** - still accurate.\n\n3. **`nonComponentItems` / `componentCounts`** - Clear separation of block-price components from individually priced items. **Keep**.\n\n4. **Constants** - All well-named: `ITEM_PRICES`, `CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE`, `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE`, `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD`, `BLOCK_PRICE_COMPONENTS`, `BLOCK_PRICE`, `LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE`, `LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD`, `EXACT_BLOCK_COUNT`, `PROCESSING_FEE`. **Keep** — all reveal intent clearly.\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\n```\nCurrent Code Mass (claim-office.ts):\n\n=== Constants (mass 1 each) ===\nITEM_PRICES (object literal with 6 entries)\n\"sword\" = 100, \"amulet\" = 60, \"staff\" = 80, \"potion\" = 40, \"rune\" = 25, \"moonstone\" = 25\nActually, let me count literal constants in the code:\n\nLiterals: 100, 60, 80, 40, 25, 25 (in ITEM_PRICES) = 6\n0.5 (CURSED_SURCHARGE_RATE) = 1\n0.3 (HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_SURCHARGE_RATE) = 1\n5 (HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) = 1\n\"rune\", \"moonstone\" (in BLOCK_PRICE_COMPONENTS set) = 2\n60 (BLOCK_PRICE) = 1\n0.2 (LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE) = 1\n2 (LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD) = 1\n3 (EXACT_BLOCK_COUNT) = 1\n5 (PROCESSING_FEE) = 1\nAlso: 0 in `|| 0`, 1 in increments, 0 initial for total = 3\nLiterals in comparison: item.enchantment >= 5  (5 is already counted)\nscenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= 2 (2 already counted)\ncount === 3 (3 already counted)\n\nSo total constants: 6 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 3 = 19\n\n=== Bindings (mass 1 each) ===\nscenario, item, nonComponentItems, componentCounts, item, total, basePrice, itemTotal, [type, count], results, step, basePremium\nCount: 12\n\n=== Invocations (mass 2 each) ===\nObject.entries(), BLOCK_PRICE_COMPONENTS.has(), Object.entries(componentCounts), ... (spread?), no\nActually let me recount more carefully with the actual code. Let me just compute the mass more precisely.\n\nFunction `calculateBasePremium`:\n- bindings: items, nonComponentItems, componentCounts, item (loop), item.type (not counted separately), itemType (not yet)\n- Actually let me just count line by line:\n\nitems = 1 binding\nnonComponentItems = 1 binding\ncomponentCounts = 1 binding\nfor...of loop = 1 loop (5)\n  item = loop binding (in loop header, 1)\n  BLOCK_PRICE_COMPONENTS.has(item.type) = 1 invocation (2)\n  if(...) = 1 conditional (4)\n  componentCounts[item.type] = ... = 1 assignment (6)\n  (componentCounts[item.type] || 0) = 1 invocation (OR short-circuit is a conditional, 4)\n  else = conditional branch already counted\n  nonComponentItems.push(item) = 1 invocation (2)\n\ntotal = 1 binding\nfor...of loop = 1 loop (5)\n  item = loop binding, 1\n  ITEM_PRICES[item.type] = 1 invocation (2)\n  || 0 = already counted pattern, but new instance = 1 invocation (2)  \n  basePrice (already itemTotal binding)\n  Wait, let me re-examine:\n\nLine by line:\n```\nconst calculateBasePremium = (items: Item[]): number => {\n```\nbindings: calculateBasePremium (1), items (1) = 2\n\n```\n  const nonComponentItems: Item[] = [];\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n```\nbindings: nonComponentItems (1), componentCounts (1) = 2\n\n```\n  for (const item of items) {\n```\nloop (5), binding: item (1)\n\n```\n    if (BLOCK_PRICE_COMPONENTS.has(item.type)) {\n```\ninvocation: has() (2), conditional: if (4)\n\n```\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] || 0) + 1;\n```\nassignment (6), 2 invocations: componentCounts[...] accesses, OR (||) is conditional (4), + is invocation (2), 0 is constant (1), 1 is constant (1)\n\nActually, `||` is a conditional operator (mass 4). Let me not over-count and use a more sensible approach. Let me also look at `= ... + 1` - that's an assignment and invocation.\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me use a more pragmatic counting approach focused on key elements.\n\nLet me re-examine. The APP counting guidelines say:\n- constants x 1\n- bindings x 1  \n- invocations x 2\n- conditionals x 4\n- loops x 5\n- assignments x 6\n\nLet me count the full file:\n\n**Constants (literal values)**: \n`100, 60, 80, 40, 25, 25` - 6 (in ITEM_PRICES)\n`0.5` - 1  \n`0.3` - 1\n`5` - 1 (threshold)\n`\"rune\"`, `\"moonstone\"` - 2 (set)\n`60` - 1\n`0.2` - 1\n`2` - 1\n`3` - 1\n`5` - 1 (PROCESSING_FEE)\nIn code: `0` in `|| 0` (2 instances: calculateBasePremium and processScenario at the discount threshold comparison - actually `|| 0` only in calculateBasePremium) = 1\n`1` in `+ 1` (increment) = 1\n\nTotal constants: ~20\n\n**Bindings**: \ncalculateBasePremium, processScenario, Scenario, Step, QuoteStep, ClaimStep, Item, Damage (interfaces don't count)\n\nFunction level: processScenario(scenario), calculateBasePremium(items)\nInside calculateBasePremium: nonComponentItems, componentCounts, item, total, basePrice, itemTotal, [type, count], count\nInside processScenario: results, step, basePremium\n\nTotal bindings: ~13\n\n**Invocations**: \nBLOCK_PRICE_COMPONENTS.has(), nonComponentItems.push(), ITEM_PRICES[...], Object.entries(), calculateBasePremium(), += (x4), -=,  * (x3 multiplication), >= (x2), - (subtraction for discount), etc.\n\nTotal invocations: ~15\n\n**Conditionals**: \nif (cursed), if (enchantment >= threshold), if (count === 3) else, if (loyalty qualified), ?: (none), for...if in classify\n\nLet me be more precise. Actually count:\n\n```\ncalculateBasePremium:\n  if (BLOCK_PRICE_COMPONENTS.has(item.type)) - 1\n  if (item.cursed) - 1  \n  if (item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD) - 1\n  if (count === EXACT_BLOCK_COUNT) [else branch] - 1\n\nprocessScenario:\n  if (step.op === \"quote\") - 1\n  if (scenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD) - 1\n\nTotal conditionals: 6\n```\n\n**Loops**:\nfor (const item of items) - 1\nfor (const item of nonComponentItems) - 1  \nfor (const [type, count] of Object.entries(componentCounts)) - 1\nfor (const step of scenario.steps) - 1\n\nTotal loops: 4\n\n**Assignments**:\ncomponentCounts[item.type] = ... + 1 - 1\nlet total = 0 - 1 (initialization with let)\nlet itemTotal = basePrice - 1\nitemTotal += basePrice * ... - 1\nitemTotal += basePrice * ... - 1 (2 more assignments)\ntotal += itemTotal - 1\ntotal += BLOCK_PRICE - 1\ntotal += count * ... - 1\nlet basePremium = ... - 1\nbasePremium -= ... - 1\n\nTotal assignments: ~9\n\n**Mass calculation**:\n- Constants: 20 × 1 = 20\n- Bindings: 12 × 1 = 12\n- Invocations: 15 × 2 = 30\n- Conditionals: 6 × 4 = 24\n- Loops: 4 × 5 = 20\n- Assignments: 10 × 6 = 60\n\n**Total Mass: 166**\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n\nLooking at the code more carefully for clarity improvements:\n\n1. The `calculateBasePremium` function now has a split personality — it classifies items and computes prices. There's a clear separation between item classification and pricing calculation. Could extract a helper.\n\n2. The constant `HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD` used with `>=` (item.enchantment >= HIGH_ENCHANTMENT_THRESHOLD). The test for enchantment 4 (NOT applied) confirms `>= 5`. Clear.\n\n3. Looking for an improvement: The `nonComponentItems` naming is long and awkward, but it's accurate and only used once. Could rename to `pricedItems` but that's more ambiguous. **Keep**.\n\n4. Actually, I notice: the classification loop separates items into components and non-components, then the pricing loop only processes non-component items, and then a separate loop for component blocks. This is clean but there's a concept repeated: three separate loops could be collapsed. But that would hurt clarity (Rule 2). **Keep as is**.\n\n### Rule 3 — No Duplication\n\nLooking for duplication:\n\n1. `ITEM_PRICES[item.type] || 0` appears in calculateBasePremium but only once. Not duplicated.\n\n2. The `+ PROCESSING_FEE` pattern is hardcoded in `results.push(...)`. If we need it elsewhere it'd be duplicated, but right now it's only one location.\n\n3. The `+ 1` pattern in `componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] || 0) + 1` is a single instance.\n\n4. No obvious duplication.  \n\n### Rule 4 — Fewest Elements\n\n1. The `componentCounts` object could be a `Map` but that's more elements, not fewer.\n\n2. The two separate loops for nonComponents and components could be merged, but the classification loop is necessary since we need to group components first before computing blocks. This is already minimal.\n\n3. **Potential improvement**: The `processScenario` function checks `if (step.op === \"quote\")` — there's no `else` for claim handling yet (all claim tests are `it.todo`). This is fine — the code only handles what's needed.\n\n4. The loyalty discount is computed inline:\n```typescript\nif (scenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD) {\n  basePremium -= basePremium * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n}\n```\n\nThe `basePremium -= basePremium * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE` could be simplified to `basePremium *= (1 - LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE)` but that is less clear about what's happening (it's a discount applied to the base). **Keep as is for clarity**.\n\n### Actual Improvement Opportunity\n\nLooking more carefully, I see one improvement:\n\nThe `TOTAL` constant is computed as `total += ...` but we return `total`. That's fine. But the `PROCESSING_FEE` is added in `processScenario`, not in `calculateAmount`. This is correct behavior but means `calculateBasePremium` doesn't include the fee.\n\nOne potential improvement: **Extract variable for the discount application to improve clarity**. Currently:\n\n```typescript\nif (scenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD) {\n  basePremium -= basePremium * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE; \n}\n```\n\nThis is actually fairly clear already. Let me think about another improvement.\n\nLooking at the constant definitions — could group them better semantically. But that's cosmetic, not structural.\n\n**Let me look for a real structural improvement**: The `calculateBasePremium` function does three things:\n1. Classify items (component vs non-component)\n2. Price non-component items (with modifiers)\n3. Price component items (with block logic)\n\nThe classification and component pricing are quite different concerns from non-component sprinting. Could separate concerns slightly for better readability.\n\nBut actually, the most impactful refactoring I see is: **the code is already quite clean**. Let me verify my APP count and look for a small but meaningful improvement.\n\n**Looking at the return in `processScenario`**: \n```typescript\nresults.push({ premium: base(input + PROCESSING_FEE });\n```\n\nThis adds the processing fee only in the quote case. The only step type currently handled is \"quotation\", so the if/else is minimal. But for future evolution (claim handling), the fee logic is tied to the quote step. This is correct.\n\nAfter thorough analysis, I believe there is one potential improvement: **rename `basePremium` inside `processScenario`**. The name `basePremium` is reused inside `processScenario` when the variable `base` is assigned from `calculateBasePremium()`, then potentially modified by loyalty. The name `basePremium` is also used to refer to the result of `calculateBasePremium` — but after modification, it's no longer the \"base\" premium. Let me reconsider: the `let basePremium` falls to the result of `calculateBasePremium`, which is correct. Then, if loyalty is applied, `basePremium` gets the discount applied. So `basePremium` starts as the actual base, then transforms into the \"premium after policy modifier\". More accurate: rename `basePremium` to `premiumAfterModifiers` or similar? Actually no — the base is the mined base, and loyalty changes it. The new value is still \"the premium\" (after policy modifiers). Perhaps `premium` would be slightly better than `based`.\n\nActually, on reflection, I think there's a clearer improvement: **rename `basePremium` to just `premium`** in `processScenario`. Here's why:\n\n```typescript\nlet basePremium = calculateBasePremium(step.items);\nif (scenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD) {\n  basePremium -= basePremium * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n}\nresults.push({ premium: basePremium + PROCESSING_FEE });\n```\n\nAfter the discount, it's no longer the \"base\" premium — it's the adjusted premium. Renaming to `premium` simplifies this and reveals intent better. This is a Rule 2 improvement.\n\nBut wait — `basePremium` at the assignment point IS the base premium. It then gets modified. The name `premium` before the discount would be slightly inaccurate. What about `quote` or `premiumBeforeFee`?\n\nActually, I think the clearest solution is: `quotePremium`. It's the premium for the quote, before the processing fee is added.\n\nLet me implement this change.\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refactoring\n\nRenaming `basePremium` → `quotePremium` in `processScenario"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_bc1511b9862f4ca5889b66aa","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_03-18-36_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi_deepseek-v4-pro-no-thinking/src/claim-office.ts","edits":[{"newText":"      let quotePremium = calculateBasePremium(step.items);\n      if (scenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD) {\n        quotePremium -= quotePremium * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n      }\n      results.push({ premium: quotePremium + PROCESSING_FEE });","oldText":"      let basePremium = calculateBasePremium(step.items);\n      if (scenario.customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_YEARS_THRESHOLD) {\n        basePremium -= basePremium * LOYALTY_DISCOUNT_RATE;\n      }\n      results.push({ premium: basePremium + PROCESSING_FEE });"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":549,"output":3960,"cacheRead":9984,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":14493,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":17849