sonable — it processes a scenario with steps. However, looking at the data returned, we're only handling quotes so far and producing premium results. The name is generic but acceptable for an evolving function.\n\n- Current name: `calculateQuote`\n- Function purpose: \"Calculates baseSum and itemSurcharges from items, applying block pricing for runes/moonstones\"\n- Assessment: Good name — it calculates a quote's financial components.\n\n**Decision**: Keep current names. `processScenario` accurately describes processing a sequence of steps in a scenario. `calculateQuote` accurately describes computing quote components. The names reveal intent adequately.\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\nLet me calculate the APP mass of `processScenario` and `calculateQuote`:\n\n### `calculateQuote` Mass:\n```typescript\nconst calculateQuote = (items: Item[]): { baseSum: number; itemSurcharges: number } => {\n  const nonComponentItems: Item[] = [];              // binding (1)\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {}; // binding (1)\n\n  for (const item of items) {                         // loop (5), binding 'item' (1)\n    if (BLOCK_PRICE_COMPONENTS.has(item.type)) {      // conditional (4), invocation (2)\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] || 0) + 1; // assignment (6), invocation (2), constant 0 (1)\n    } else {\n      nonComponentItems.push(item);                   // invocation (2)\n    }\n  }\n\n  let baseSum = 0;                                    // binding (1), constant 0 (1)\n  let itemSurcharges = 0;                            // binding (1), constant 0 (1)\n\n  for (const item of nonComponentItems) {            // loop (5), binding 'item' (1)\n    const basePrice = ITEM_PRICES[item.type] || 0;   // binding (1), invocation (2), constant 0 (1)\n    baseSum += basePrice;                            // assignment (6)\n    if (item.cursed) {                               // conditional (4)\n      itemSurcharges += base