 This is a new operation that needs significant implementation. Let me start.\n\n## Red -- Test 24: Standard reimbursement — steel sword enchant 3 damage 500 → 400 G\n\nFirst, I need to understand the claim process. The claim step references a policy by index. The spec says:\n\n\"Claim processing: A deductible of 100 G applies per damage event. The total payout per policy is capped at twice the insurance sum.\"\n\nFor the claim test, I need the processScenario to handle claim steps. The claim result should include `payout` (integer) and `remainingCap` (integer).\n\nFor this test: sword insured with quote. Then claim step. Insurance sum = 1000 G. Cap = 2000 G. Damage = 500 G. Deductible = 100 G per damage item (per damage event? No: \"per damage event\"). \"Damage to items with enchantment level ≥ 8 is reimbursed at 50%. Damage to items made of dragon material is fully reimbursed.\"\n\nFor a steel sword enchant 3: no special clauses. Reimbursement = full amount - deductible = 500 - 100 = 400. Payout = 400. Remaining cap = 2000 - 400 = 1600.\n\nLet me write this test. But I need the full scenario structure — the claim references the quote step index.\n\n\n\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_91888d656f804bdc9e930ff5","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"  it(\"should reimburse 400 G for steel sword enchant 3 damaged 500 G\", () => {\n    const scenario = {\n      customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n      steps: [\n        { op: \"quote\" as const, items: [{ type: \"sword\", material: \"steel\", enchantment: 3 }] },\n        { op: \"claim\" as const, policy: 0, incident: { cause: \"dragon\", damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: 500 }] } }\n      ]\n    };\n    const result = processScenario(scenario);\n    expect(result).toEqual({ results: [{ premium: expect.any(Number) as number }, { payout: 400, remainingCap: 1600 }] });\n  });","oldText":"  it.todo(\"should reimburse 400 G for steel sword enchant 3 damaged 500 G\");"}],"path":"/home/experimenter/exper